# Addon Structure

- Treat an addon as the feature boundary. Keep its API, GUI, migrations, jobs, tests, and runtime-specific dependencies together instead of editing the generic host runtime.
- Before inventing shared substrate (tags, search, comments, flags, webhooks, mail, …), check `framework-platform-capabilities` and import only documented paths (`framework-public-imports`).
- Every API addon must have root `__manifest__.py` and `__init__.py` files. A GUI contribution additionally uses a root `index.js`.
- Define a `MANIFEST` dictionary. Use a stable `name` matching the addon directory, and include appropriate `classification`, `title`, `version`, `author`, `description`, and `depends` metadata.
- Required `classification` is exactly one of: `base` (framework-owned addons under `framework/addons/`), `enterprise` (custom addons intended for enterprise deployments), or `community` (custom addons for non-enterprise / recreational deployments). Discovery rejects missing or unknown values. Do not invent additional classification strings.
- **Manifest classification is the source of truth.** Operator checkouts may group custom addons under layout buckets such as `addons/enterprise/<name>/` and `addons/community/<name>/`, or keep a flat `addons/<name>/`. Buckets are not addons (no `__manifest__.py`). Discovery walks recursively for leaf packages; runtime identity and Python/GUI imports stay `addons.<name>` / `@addons/<name>` regardless of nesting. Do not encode bucket names into import paths.
- Use `depends`, not the legacy `dependencies` alias. Declare every addon whose classes, models, components, or registration behavior the addon requires; never rely on incidental discovery order.
- Removability contract: removing addon `X` must not break anything outside `X`'s reverse required-dependency closure. Cascade is intentional—dependents of `X` must be absent when `X` is absent. `system` is the substrate exception.
- Direct Python imports of another addon require that addon in direct `depends`. Transitive availability is not enough.
- Framework and `system` code must not enumerate other addons by name. Specific
  custom-addon identities are forbidden everywhere in the tracked framework
  repository — imports, strings, comments, **and** identifiers/API purpose —
  as defined by `framework-custom-addon-isolation.mdc`. When a shared surface
  needs per-addon presentation metadata such as an icon, label, or ordering,
  define a component the owning addon declares, and keep a generic fallback for
  undeclared cases. A hardcoded name map silently degrades whenever an addon is
  added or renamed. Custom-addon sessions keep edits under the owning leaf
  (`addons/<name>/` or `addons/<bucket>/<name>/`) unless a justified generic
  contract must land in the framework.
- **Renaming an addon** is a code change **plus** a data-plane cutover. After
  directory/manifest/constant renames, run
  `scripts/addon/check_addon_rename_cutover.py --from <old> --to <new>` and clear
  remaining DB/S3 identity before calling the rename done. See
  `framework-addon-data-lifecycle` (rename cutover).
- Prefer soft edges for maximum modularity: use `optional_depends` plus deferred, guarded imports (never top-level) when a peer enhances rather than defines core behavior. Escalate to hard `depends` only when the edge is intrinsically required. Most hard depends target framework base/substrate addons—that is expected—but the same prefer-optional rule of thumb applies among base addons when a soft edge is honest. Prefer a glue addon when the optional surface grows beyond a narrow adapter. When shared agnostic capability is material and multi-consumer, prefer extracting or extending a `framework/addons/` substrate (Substrate Check) over copying logic across leaves — see `framework-addon-hygiene-checks` and `framework-addon-substrate`. For intentional modularity/removability reviews, follow `framework-addon-modularity`. For isolation audits, follow `framework-addon-isolation`.
- **Cache Leverage Check** (new addon or substantial new/reworked expensive shared reads): decide whether catalogs, pure snapshots, or other stable shared values should use the `cache` addon via `optional_depends` and a deferred guarded bridge that fails open to compute-fresh. Do not cache full page JSON, per-viewer enrichment, pending/settle flags, or high-churn status. Authority: `framework-cache-contracts` and `docs/sphinx/source/addon-cache.md`. Skip the check on trivial local edits.
- **Legal contribution check** (new addon or substantial feature that users must agree to, or that collects personal data beyond baseline): prefer contributing `BaseLegalSection` clauses (and `BaseLegalCategory` when needed) **in the owning addon** via the `legal` contract (`depends: ["legal"]`) while building the feature. Do **not** put product clauses into `framework/addons/legal`, and do **not** force empty ToS/Privacy stubs on every addon. Attach to baseline category ids when they fit; otherwise publish an addon-owned category. Same for Privacy as for Terms. Authority: `docs/sphinx/source/addon-legal.md`.
- Keep dependency graphs acyclic. Dependencies control addon load order; component `registration_phase` and `sequence` control ordering inside that graph.
- Use `api/`, `gui/`, and `mcp/` as runtime boundaries: `api/` is loaded by the
  API (and worker/beat) runtime, `gui/` by the GUI runtime, and `mcp/` by the
  MCP runtime. Do not import Python implementation into browser code or move
  browser decisions into backend serializers. Addon root `__init__.py` must not
  import `mcp/` so API hosts never pull FastMCP tool registration.
- Make root entrypoints explicit. Root `__init__.py` stays import-neutral (no
  eager `api` / `mcp` import) so MCP can load without Flask. API discovery
  imports `{addon}.api`; `api/__init__.py` imports component-bearing and runtime
  packages; component package initializers import every family that discovery
  must scan.
- Keep entrypoint exports intentional; use `__all__` where it clarifies the public Python surface. Discovery depends on modules being imported, not on `__all__`.
- Create only the component-family directories the addon needs. Do not reproduce the full reference layout as empty scaffolding.
- Declare Python dependencies in addon `requirements.txt` files and browser dependencies in addon `package.json` files. Add compatibility bounds when behavior depends on specific versions.
- Keep addon tests beside the owning runtime code, in `api/tests/`, `gui/tests/`,
  and `mcp/tests/` for the runtimes the addon contributes to. Never place an
  addon's tests under `framework/` or invoke them from framework workflows:
  nothing in the tracked repository may import an `addons/` package or a
  `@addons/<product addon>` path. Deleting the addon must delete its
  tests too, and every framework suite must stay green with no product addon
  installed. `scripts/addon/check_addon_dependencies.py` enforces this.
- Do not import reverse-direction addon packages in tests; put cross-addon assertions in the consumer addon. Add focused coverage for the discovery, migration, payload, or renderer contracts the addon changes.
